/**
 * The current account's saved Reader posts.
 *
 * Saves belong to the account rather than a site, so this is separate from `WpService`. It keeps
 * everything in the cache, under the account: a save or unsave shows straight away and survives a
 * relaunch, and one the server hasn't got yet is sent by a later call. Call [`Self::sync`] at
 * launch, when the app comes to the foreground, and when the device comes back online.
 *
 * [`WpApiCache::remove_wordpress_com_account`] deletes it all, except the stored image files: see
 * [`Self::remove_offline_images`].
 *
 * Create one per account and share it: it sends one request at a time, and a second instance
 * would send alongside it.
 */
public protocol ReaderSavedServiceProtocol: AnyObject, Sendable {
    
    /**
     * How many saves and unsaves the server doesn't have yet: the ones waiting to be sent, and
     * the saves waiting for room. A signed-out reader's saves all count: no server has them.
     */
    func pendingCount() throws  -> UInt64
    
    /**
     * Saves a post. It reads as saved, and shows in the saved list, straight away.
     */
    func save(post: ReaderPostSaveIdentity) async throws  -> ReaderSavedOutcome
    
    /**
     * Saves a page by URL. The URL of a WordPress.com post is saved as the post.
     */
    func saveUrl(url: String, pageDetails: ReaderSavedPageDetails?) async throws  -> ReaderSavedOutcome
    
    /**
     * Saves a post the app already has, such as one from a Reader stream or the post screen.
     *
     * Like [`Self::save`], but the post's details are stored in the same transaction as the save,
     * so the saved list shows the post in full straight away, offline too, rather than only its key
     * until the next refresh. They only fill in what the cache doesn't have yet: what the server
     * sent for the post wins, and so does what the saved list sends later. Leave out a field the app
     * doesn't have rather than guessing it: `tags` is `None` when the app doesn't know them, and
     * empty only when the post has none.
     *
     * If the server refuses the save, it's undone as with [`Self::save`], and the details stay in
     * the cache, where the saved list doesn't show them.
     */
    func saveWithDetails(post: ReaderPost) async throws  -> ReaderSavedOutcome
    
    /**
     * How many saves there are, or `None` until the first refresh or answer. A post saved under
     * two keys counts twice, as the server counts it.
     */
    func savedCount() throws  -> UInt64?
    
    /**
     * How many saves wait for room under the account's save cap. They read as saved, and are sent
     * once the user unsaves enough to make room.
     */
    func savesWaitingForRoom() throws  -> UInt64
    
    func setObserver(observer: ReaderSavedObserver?) 
    
    /**
     * Whether a post is saved. Doesn't touch the network.
     */
    func status(post: ReaderPostSaveIdentity) throws  -> ReaderSavedStatus
    
    /**
     * Sends what the server doesn't have yet, then reloads the saved keys. Call it at launch, when
     * the app comes to the foreground, and when the device comes back online.
     *
     * Returns [`ReaderSavedError::TooManySaves`] after doing all that if saves are still waiting
     * for room under the account's save cap.
     */
    func sync() async throws 
    
    /**
     * Unsaves a post from every key it may be saved under. It reads as unsaved straight away.
     */
    func unsave(post: ReaderPostSaveIdentity) async throws  -> ReaderSavedOutcome
    
    /**
     * Unsaves a key, such as a saved list item's. If the server stores it as another key, that's
     * unsaved too.
     */
    func unsaveKey(key: ReaderSavedKey) async throws  -> ReaderSavedOutcome
    
    /**
     * Gets every saved post ready to read with no connection. Call it where [`Self::sync`] is
     * called, when there's a connection worth using: it syncs first.
     *
     * It fetches the content of each save that has none, by loading the saved list as far as it
     * needs to and then fetching what's left one post at a time, however many that is: one call
     * leaves every save with its content, or with the reason it has none. Each save is tried at
     * most once a call, so a call sends at most one request for each save, after the list. A save
     * the server can't give the content of reads as [`ReaderOfflineState::Failed`], and is tried
     * again an hour later, five times at most.
     *
     * Saves the list doesn't send, such as many imported at once, each take a request, so a call
     * can take a while. It holds the saved list's turn throughout: a save or unsave made meanwhile
     * shows at once, and is sent when the download ends.
     *
     * With `include_images`, and once [`Self::set_offline_image_store`] has said where, it then
     * stores the images of each saved post, newest save first. When they'd take more room than
     * allowed, the oldest saves' images are removed to fit a newer save's, and only when all of
     * that save's then fit. Whether or not it includes images, it ends by fitting the stored
     * images into the room, as [`Self::set_offline_image_store`] does.
     *
     * A failure to reach the server isn't an error here: the download stops there, keeps what
     * it fetched, and the report says why it stopped and what the device has so far.
     */
    func downloadForOffline(includeImages: Bool) async throws  -> ReaderOfflineReport
    
    /**
     * How much room the stored images take, in bytes. It counts every reader's that share the
     * cache.
     */
    func offlineImageBytes() throws  -> UInt64
    
    /**
     * A saved post for reading with no connection, or `None` if the key isn't saved or the
     * cache has no post for it.
     *
     * Each stored image in its content points at `image_url_prefix` followed by the file's
     * name, and so do [`ReaderPostRecord::featured_image`] and
     * [`ReaderPostRecord::display_image_url`] when they're stored. Pass the directory's `file:`
     * address with a `/` at the end, or the prefix of a scheme the app's web view serves the
     * files through. An image that isn't stored keeps its own address.
     */
    func offlinePost(key: ReaderSavedKey, imageUrlPrefix: String) throws  -> ReaderOfflinePost?
    
    /**
     * Deletes every stored image of this reader's saved posts, and stops storing them until
     * [`Self::set_offline_image_store`] is called again. The posts' text stays.
     *
     * At sign-out, call it with the store set before
     * [`wp_mobile_cache::WpApiCache::remove_wordpress_com_account`], so the files go at once.
     * Otherwise they go the next time a reader on the cache sets its store or downloads images.
     */
    func removeOfflineImages() throws 
    
    /**
     * Tries again, now, to fetch the content of a save that
     * [`ReaderOfflineState::Failed`], whatever its earlier tries. Returns what it is afterwards.
     * Fails with [`ReaderSavedError::OfflineUnreachable`] if the server can't be reached, and with
     * the server's [`ReaderSavedError::Api`] error if it rejects the account's token. Neither
     * counts against the save.
     */
    func retryOffline(key: ReaderSavedKey) async throws  -> ReaderOfflineState
    
    /**
     * Says where to keep saved posts' images, and how much room they may take, in bytes. Until
     * it's called, only posts' text is kept.
     *
     * `directory` is created if it isn't there. Give one that is only for these files and isn't
     * cleared by the system, and give the same one each launch: the files are found by name.
     * Readers on one cache can share it, and `max_bytes` counts every reader's images. Nothing
     * is fetched until [`Self::download_for_offline`] is asked to include images.
     *
     * When the stored images take more than `max_bytes`, such as after the app gives less room
     * than before, it deletes this reader's oldest saves' images, each post's all together,
     * until they fit or this reader has none left. The posts keep their text, and a download
     * with more room fetches their images again. It also deletes the files in `directory` that
     * no reader's saved post has any more, such as the images of an account removed with
     * [`wp_mobile_cache::WpApiCache::remove_wordpress_com_account`].
     *
     * If deleting fails, it returns the error, and the store is set all the same. The next
     * download deletes what's left over.
     */
    func setOfflineImageStore(directory: String, maxBytes: UInt64) throws 
    
    /**
     * Takes over the saves made on this device while nobody was signed in, as an import: each is
     * saved to this account, with what the device kept of its post, and the signed-out reader's
     * saved list is emptied. Call it after sign-in, on the account's service. A post the account
     * already saved stays as it is.
     *
     * When the account has room for only some of them under its save cap, which ones are saved
     * is up to the server: with room for one of two, it took the newer. The rest stay saved on
     * the device and wait for room.
     */
    func adoptSignedOutSaves() async throws  -> ReaderSavedImportReport
    
    /**
     * Imports saves, given newest first, such as a device's saves from before saves were synced
     * or a list the app read from a file. Each is a save: a post already saved stays as it is,
     * and one unsaved is saved again, so importing the same list twice changes nothing. Call it
     * whenever there's a list to import.
     *
     * The saves are in the cache before anything is sent, so they show in the saved list, and a
     * later sync sends any this one couldn't. A save the server refuses is undone, and listed in
     * [`ReaderSavedImportReport::rejected`]. One past the account's save cap stays saved instead,
     * and is sent once there's room: see [`Self::saves_waiting_for_room`].
     */
    func importSaves(saves: [ReaderLocalSave]) async throws  -> ReaderSavedImportReport
    
    /**
     * The saved list, from the cache, newest save first.
     */
    func savedPosts()  -> ReaderSavedPostsCollection
    
    /**
     * The saved list in another order: by when each post was saved or published, newest or
     * oldest first. A save without that date comes last.
     *
     * It's the same saves, read from the cache in that order. Only loading differs: each order
     * loads its own pages from the server, so the list in one order may be further along than
     * in another.
     */
    func savedPostsOrdered(orderBy: ReaderSavedOrderBy, order: ReaderStreamOrder)  -> ReaderSavedPostsCollection
    
}
